\title{Union-find analysis}
\date{2026-10-15T16:11:27Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{How long do these operations take?  Clearly \sf{Union} takes #{T_{\sf{Find}} + \Theta(1)}. So implementing \sf{Find} efficiently is the crux of the issue.  It seems like it might take #{\Theta(n)} in the worst case, if we end up with an unbalanced tree—for example, if all the items in a tree end up in a long linear chain.  But it turns out that being careful in how we implement \sf{Union}, taking heights into account, means this will never happen!  The following theorem tells us something about what the resulting trees can look like.}

\transclude{00PG}   % Union-find trees are balanced
\transclude{00PH}   % Cor: find + union take lg n time
